ether the order is plausible, never whether exact frequency can be verified.\n- Rare: specialist, technical, legal, archaic, and rare senses, and senses used only in a variety other than British or American English, belong in extra (\"stay\" meaning to stop legal proceedings, \"mobile\" meaning changing facial expression, Scottish \"stay\" meaning to live somewhere), and every sense in extra is one a learner does not meet in general use.\nA rare, archaic, Old English, technical, or specialist sense missing from both lists is never a failure.\nfail when any of them does not hold. The reason then names everything to change, even if that takes more than one sentence: each missing common sense with its part of speech and a short meaning, each pair of duplicates by number, each sense out of order, each specialist sense in common, and each sense in extra that is common.\nuncertain: only when you cannot judge the word at all. Never answer uncertain because the exact frequency order cannot be verified: a plausible order passes."}}}], "rescored": true}, "context": {"subject": {"lemma": "hot", "senses": [{"id": "98cdca81-7f89-42aa-85c5-fd6d3f57fe24", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Having a high temperature or a temperature higher than is comfortable."}, {"id": "16750b2c-f8a8-4507-b4a4-03783943275a", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Recent enough to provide promising clues about someone or something being sought."}, {"id": "2cb3f0a5-6141-4003-a231-aab994fb1cd3", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Very close to finding a hidden object or guessing the correct answer in a game."}, {"id": "94c15bba-939d-4361-b023-b2670fa6d093", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Having a strong taste that causes a burning feeling in the mouth."}, {"id": "e85f4f2d-a8ab-436e-8a78-ced0b44b5f0b", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Very popular, fashionable, or in great demand."}, {"id": "b108ed5f-d185-4405-ba3f-7b1974e7055a", "pos": "adjective", "gloss": "Causing strong disagreement, excitement, or public interest."}, {